UNIT sir_parse
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs — parse(text) in its default legacy mode; the code is the truth, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG sir_parse(text: text) -> json
ENVELOPE any well-formed text (every character, every line-break style, a byte-order mark anywhere). The parser never throws
  on text; the strict mode and the indentation statistics of the same function are OUT (SCOPE).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Parse SIR source text into a map with three entries: "ast" (a list of nodes, one per directive line plus one per
  comment line met before the first directive), "errors" and "warnings" (lists of diagnostics, in line order). A directive
  node is a map with the entries "directive" and "name" (both the directive name), "args" (text), "attrs" (a map from
  attribute keyword to text), "attrList" (a list of [keyword, value] pairs, every attribute occurrence in order), "cont"
  (a list of continuation texts), "comments" (a list of texts), "line" (its line number), "register" (text or none) and
  "kind" (text). A diagnostic is a map with "line", "code" and, for the codes TABLE DIAGNOSTICS marks, "msg". Legacy mode
  tolerates comments (reporting each), colon-suffixed names and unindented prose. SPACE means a space or a tab, and only
  those two characters: every separator, indentation, trim and blank test in this contract is over SPACE.
BEHAVIOR
R1 LINES — the same lines canonical_text binds. The text is cut at every CR LF pair, every lone CR and every lone LF; line n
  (counting from 1) is the n-th piece, so a text ending in a break has a final empty line and the empty text is one empty
  line. Each line's LEAD is its longest prefix made only of SPACE and BOM characters; remove EVERY BOM from the LEAD,
  keeping its SPACE characters in their order (so BOM SPACE BOM TAB x becomes SPACE TAB x, and the result begins with
  no BOM after its SPACE run: one pass is a fixed point); then remove the line's trailing run of SPACE. A BOM after the
  first character that is neither SPACE nor BOM stays content (NBSP BOM x keeps it). Line numbers in every output are these numbers.
  CITED canonical_text — the shared splitter
R2 Lines are processed in order against CURRENT, the directive node that receives continuations; CURRENT is none at the
  start and only R7 changes it. A line that R1 left empty (so a line of only SPACE) is skipped: no node, no diagnostic.
  Every other line is content, including one made only of NBSP, U+3000, U+2028 or any other non-SPACE whitespace.
R3 INDENT = the line's leading run of SPACE; CONTENT = the rest. INDENT empty = column 0.
R4 FULL-LINE COMMENT: CONTENT begins with "#" (at column 0 or indented) -> warning W_LEGACY_COMMENT, and nothing else
  happens to the line beyond this: with a CURRENT, its "comments" gets CONTENT minus its whole leading run of "#" and then
  minus ONE following SPACE character when there is one; with no CURRENT, a comment node is appended to the ast:
  "directive" and "name" "#", "body" CONTENT, "args" "", "attrs" empty, "attrList" empty, "cont" empty, "comments"
  [CONTENT], "line", "register" none, "kind" "comment". A comment node never becomes CURRENT.
R5 QUOTED SPANS of a text S (the one literal rule; R6 applies it to CONTENT, R11 to its T). A double quote is an OPENER when
  it starts a token: it is the first character of S, or the character right before it is in the pinned set OPENER-PREV,
  or it stands directly after the closing quote of a span (so two adjacent literals are two spans). Any other predecessor
  — a letter, a digit, "/", a backslash, "#", ".", "*", "+", "?", a closing bracket, NBSP, a quote that does not close a
  span — makes it plain text: an inch mark after a digit, a quote inside a slash pattern or after a regex quantifier
  opens nothing. A closing quote need not end a token: after "a"b the next quote follows "b" and opens nothing. Scanning left to right, from
  an opener read on to the next double quote that is not escaped, where an ESCAPE PAIR is a backslash together with the
  one character (one Unicode scalar value, U+2028 and astral characters included) that follows it. When that closing
  quote exists the span runs from the opener to it, and the scan resumes after the closing quote; when it does not (the
  end of S comes first — a final backslash pairs with nothing and also reaches the end) the opener is UNCLOSED, opens no
  span and protects nothing. A position is INSIDE a span when it lies strictly between its opener and its closing quote. Single quotes and pattern slashes never delimit CITED FIDELITY package
R6 TRAILING COMMENT: otherwise, the MARKER is the first "#" of CONTENT that is immediately preceded by a SPACE character,
  immediately followed by a SPACE character or by the end of CONTENT, and not inside a quoted span of CONTENT (R5). With
  a MARKER: CODE = the CONTENT before the "#", its trailing SPACE run removed; COMMENT = the CONTENT after the "#" with
  SPACE trimmed from both ends (possibly empty); warning W_LEGACY_TRAILING_COMMENT (always this line's first
  diagnostic). Without: CODE = CONTENT and there is no COMMENT.
R7 HEAD (column 0 only): NAME = CODE up to its first SPACE character (all of CODE when it has none); REST = the rest of
  CODE, starting WITH that SPACE character ("" when CODE has none). A name is DIRECTIVE-SHAPED when it is an uppercase
  ASCII letter followed by any number of uppercase ASCII letters, ASCII digits and "-". When NAME is directive-shaped
  followed by one final ":" and the part before the ":" is not "CITED", the ":" is dropped from NAME (CODE and REST are
  unchanged) and warning W_LEGACY_COLON with msg = NAME is reported; "CITED:" keeps its colon, is not a directive and
  draws no W_LEGACY_COLON. The line is a DIRECTIVE line when NAME is now directive-shaped and is not "CITED". Its
  register and kind come from TABLE REGISTRY by exact NAME; a NAME absent from it gets register none, kind "unknown", and
  warning W_UNKNOWN_DIRECTIVE with msg NAME. ATTRS(REST) (R11) gives "args" (the body) and the node's attributes, stored
  in group order (R12); "cont" is empty; "comments" is [COMMENT] when the line has one, else empty; "line" is the line
  number. The node is appended to the ast and becomes CURRENT. Because REST keeps its leading SPACE, a keyword first on
  the line IS an attribute ("SIG CITED x" has args "" and CITED "x").
R8 NAME SEPARATOR (column 0, not a directive line; at most one warning W_NAME_SEPARATOR). HEX(c) = "U+" then the code
  point of character c in uppercase hexadecimal padded with leading zeros to at least four digits (five for an astral
  one). A WORD is an uppercase ASCII letter and the longest following run of uppercase ASCII letters, ASCII digits and "-".
  (a) GLUED: CODE begins with a WORD G, then optionally one ":", then a character C of the pinned set GLUE -> msg = G, " is
  followed by ", HEX(C), ", not a space or tab: not ", then "an attribute" when G is "CITED", else "a directive".
  (b) INVISIBLE LEAD, only when (a) does not apply: CODE begins with a maximal run of one or more GLUE characters, and the
  character after the run is "#" or begins a WORD W that is itself followed by a SPACE character, a ":", a GLUE character
  or the end of CODE -> msg = "the line starts with ", HEX(the first character of CODE), " (whitespace or an invisible
  character) before ", then "a comment marker: not a comment" after a "#", else "a directive-shaped name: not " and
  "an attribute" when W is "CITED", else "a directive". The line then continues as R9 says, whether or not R8 reported. CITED FIDELITY package
R9 PROSE (column 0, not a directive line): with a CURRENT, CONTINUE(CODE, COMMENT) (R10) and then warning
  W_UNINDENTED_CONTINUATION — so a column-0 CITED (and "CITED:") attaches to the directive above. With no CURRENT: error
  E_NO_DIRECTIVE, msg = the first 40 characters of CODE (all of it when shorter; a character is one Unicode scalar value,
  so an emoji counts once and is never cut); the line's COMMENT is discarded.
R10 INDENTED line (INDENT non-empty, not R4): with no CURRENT -> error E_ORPHAN_CONTINUATION, msg = the first 40 characters
  of CODE, and nothing is stored. With a CURRENT, CONTINUE(CODE, COMMENT). CONTINUE: ATTRS of the text one space followed
  by CODE; its attributes are stored into CURRENT (R12); its body is appended to CURRENT's "cont" only when non-empty;
  COMMENT, when present (an empty one too), is appended to CURRENT's "comments".
R11 ATTRS(T) -> (body, attribute list). A KEYWORD is "CITED", "EARNED" or "ANCHOR". A GROUP is a run of one or more SPACE
  characters, then a keyword, then a SPACE character or the end of T; groups are found left to right without overlap (the
  SPACE that ends one group may begin the next). A group whose keyword starts inside a quoted span of T (R5) is data: it
  is dropped, and only the remaining groups count below. No group -> body = T with SPACE trimmed from both ends, empty
  list. Otherwise body = the text before the first group, SPACE-trimmed, and the list holds one [keyword, value] per
  group, in order, where value = the text from the end of its keyword up to the start of the next group (or the end of
  T), SPACE-trimmed — possibly empty. A keyword next to any other character (NBSP, a letter, a ":") is plain text.
R12 STORE [k, v] into a node: append [k, v] to its "attrList"; then its "attrs" entry k = the non-empty values of every
  [k, …] in "attrList" so far, in order, joined by "; " ("" when none is non-empty; the entry exists from k's first
  occurrence). So every occurrence is kept in "attrList" across the directive line and all its continuations, and
  "CITED a; b" and "CITED a CITED b" give the same "attrs" but different "attrList" CITED FIDELITY package
R13 Output: the map {"ast", "errors", "warnings"}. Diagnostics are appended in line order, and within one line in the order
  R6, R7, R8, R9 state them. Errors come only from R9 and R10: legacy mode raises no other error.
TABLE DIAGNOSTICS — code | list | carries msg
  W_LEGACY_COMMENT           | warnings | no
  W_LEGACY_TRAILING_COMMENT  | warnings | no
  W_LEGACY_COLON             | warnings | yes, the name without its colon
  W_UNKNOWN_DIRECTIVE        | warnings | yes, the name
  W_NAME_SEPARATOR           | warnings | yes, the sentence of R8 (a) or (b)
  W_UNINDENTED_CONTINUATION  | warnings | no
  E_NO_DIRECTIVE             | errors   | yes, the first 40 characters of CODE
  E_ORPHAN_CONTINUATION      | errors   | yes, the first 40 characters of CODE
TABLE REGISTRY — the parser's DIRECTIVES data (the SIR_SCHEMA §23a vocabulary freeze), grouped by value; register | kind | names
  "B" | "lb"     | "UNIT" "SIG" "ENVELOPE" "DEPENDS-ON" "PINNED" "EARNED" "CARRIED"
  "B" | "pr"     | "DESCRIPTION" "NOTE" "RATIONALE" "PROVENANCE"
  "P" | "lb"     | "KIND" "FIDELITY" "ANCHOR" "SRC" "TRIGGER" "REACH" "ORACLE-CLASS" "TRACE-SEAM" "SEAM" "BEHAVIOR" "TABLE"
                 | "UNGUARDED-PRECONDITION" "OUT-OF-DOMAIN" "ORACLE" "SHAPE" "CANONICAL-SHA256" "AUTHORITY" "STATE" "EMIT"
                 | "REQUEST" "BRANCH" "ON-ERROR" "PAR" "RING" "LIFECYCLE" "EFFECT" "SURFACE", and every NAME that is "R"
                 | followed by one or more ASCII digits ("R0", "R12", "R007")
  "P" | "pr"     | "INVARIANT" "SCOPE"
  "P" | "legacy" | "SIR" "VARIANT" "CARRIED-DATA" "NOTES" "GUARD-PINNED"
  "L" | "lb"     | "LANG" "PRIMARY-SHA256" "ABI" "MECHANISM"
PINNED charset OPENER-PREV = the two SPACE characters and "(" "[" "{" "," "=" ":" ";" "|" "<" ">" "!" "&" "-" "~" CITED FIDELITY package — the character right before an opening quote (R5): space, tab and fourteen ASCII punctuation marks
PINNED charset GLUE = code points (hex) "000A" through "000D" "0085" "00A0" "00AD" "0600" through "0605" "061C" "06DD" "070F" "0890" "0891" "08E2" "1680" "180E" "2000" through "200F" "2028" through "202F" "205F" through "2064" "2066" through "206F" "3000" "FEFF" "FFF9" through "FFFB" "110BD" "110CD" "13430" through "1343F" "1BCA0" through "1BCA3" "1D173" through "1D17A" "E0001" "E0020" through "E007F" CITED FIDELITY package — the parser's exported GLUE: Unicode White_Space other than space and tab, plus General_Category Cf, as of Unicode 17.0, fixed in the code and never the host's tables (LF and CR cannot occur in a line); used ONLY by R8
INVARIANT the nodes of ast are in strictly increasing "line" order; every diagnostic's line is a line R2 did not skip; for
  every directive node, "attrs" has exactly the keywords that occur in "attrList".
ORACLE value -> oracles/sir_parse.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 4636e704c56de4ba5a2a42a670ebe01b8fc12ed3322822dda8c6f3453de84273
SCOPE IN: parse in its default (legacy) mode and its three outputs ast / errors / warnings. OUT: strict mode (E_COMMENT,
  E_LEGACY_COLON, E_NAME_SEPARATOR, E_UNINDENTED_CONTINUATION), the "indents" statistics map the same call returns, the
  printer, canonicalText and its hashes, get / all, non-text input (the host throws).
PROVENANCE rederive@6199e2cc8b75 (MIT), cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs, functions sirLines, quotedSpans, splitTrailingComment,
  splitAttributes, glued and parse; observed through selfhost/seams/sir_parse.mjs. Cut cold 2026-10-26 for
  REDERIVE_SELF_PLAN Tier 1; round 3 re-cut to 31803f408bef (FINDINGS #43); round 4 re-cut to 978444f3baf3 (selfhost
  FINDINGS 59 to 62): the one token-start quoted-span rule shared by the comment and attribute splits, escape pairs over any
  code point, glued names over White_Space and Cf, no colon fix for "CITED:"; round 5 re-cut to bb8ded84fa50 (selfhost
  FINDINGS 75, 76, 83): the wider OPENER-PREV set and adjacent literals, a BOM after the indent, the pinned GLUE set, glued
  "NAME:", the invisible lead, "not an attribute" for CITED; round 5b delta re-cut to 6199e2cc8b75 (selfhost FINDINGS 99,
  102, 105, 107): every BOM of the leading SPACE/BOM run, no "*" "+" "?" openers, the lead before a glued name and before
  "#", the lead message naming what follows.
